INT. ROADSIDE INN - NIGHT

A cramped room, a table, a candle. Three chairs. A bottle of rye between them.

ELIAS, fifties, a bounty hunter, mud on his boots. CORAZON, thirties, the innkeeper, sleeves rolled up. TUCK, twenty, a boy with a bandaged hand.

ELIAS
You know who I'm after.

CORAZON
I know a lot of people.

ELIAS
A man with a red scarf and a limp.

CORAZON
Plenty of limps in this valley.

ELIAS
Not plenty with a scarf.

TUCK
(quiet)
He was here Tuesday.

CORAZON
Tuck.

TUCK
He was, Mama. He paid for the room with a gold coin.

ELIAS
A gold coin. Where's the coin now?

CORAZON
In the church box.

ELIAS
Then I'll want to see the church box.

CORAZON
It's Sunday. The padre is asleep.

ELIAS
Then wake him.

TUCK
Mister, he said he was going to Albuquerque.

ELIAS
Did he say why?

TUCK
To see a doctor.

ELIAS
For the limp.

TUCK
For his heart. He said it was broken.

Elias pours a drink. Doesn't touch it.

ELIAS
He killed a family in Santa Rosa. Three children.

CORAZON
God forgive him.

ELIAS
I don't want God to. That's my job.

TUCK
Mama, tell him.

CORAZON
Tell him what?

TUCK
About the letter.

Corazon closes her eyes. Elias leans forward.

ELIAS
What letter?

CORAZON
He left a letter. Addressed to a priest in Albuquerque. I didn't mail it.

ELIAS
Why not?

CORAZON
Because I read it.

TUCK
She cried all night.

ELIAS
Ma'am. What did it say?

CORAZON
That he wanted to be found.

A long silence. The candle gutters. Elias sets his glass down.

ELIAS
Give me the letter.

CORAZON
It's mine now.

ELIAS
Ma'am.

CORAZON
Let him find his own way to the rope. Take the letter, you'll be the one who took him there.

Elias studies her. Then Tuck. Then the bottle.

ELIAS
Keep the letter. Mail it tomorrow.

CORAZON
And you?

ELIAS
I'll ride to Albuquerque ahead of it.
